"Alex O." <response@myrealbox.com> wrote:
> Janis,
>
> I am not the poster you replied to but allow me to state my point of
> view.

That's okay. This is Usenet. Here everybody talks to everybody.

> Because Nethack is not just a game, it is a community.
>
> Somebody mentioned chess, so allow me to use it as an analogy.
>
> Assume that we are both chess players that decided to look for
> variants (yes, there are quite a number of them http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chess_variant).
> I became partial to Fischer Random Chess while you preferred the
> Marseillaise Chess variant.
> Net result: while we are both enjoying our selected variants, we are
> no longer a part of the same community.  We cannot compete in the same

Both you of are not part of the same Chess variant community.

But you are still part of the Chess community.

A Slash'Em player and a SporkHack player still have a lot in common.
More than a Slash'Em player and a ZAngband player.

> tournaments on a more-or-less equal footing.  Hell, we cannot even
> discuss our respective game on the same level.

No, but you can talk about the differences of your favorite variant
between each other and the vanilla version.

Such discussion can be fruitful and lead to new ideas for features.

> Call me odd but I do not consider it a good thing.

Then you don't know the Angband community.

Circa 60 variants[1] of which about ten are actively developed.

The code flow between the variants is impressive and the flow of ideas
even more so. IMO this is a prime example of open source software
evolution.


Unfortunately NetHack doesn't have a lot variants a the moment.
SporkHack and Slash'Em are the most prominent, NetHack brass,
GruntHack, the biodiversity patch have a rather low profile judging
from this newsgroup.

Remember that during the 90s a lot of new features in vanilla NetHack
came from variants. The DevTeam does not and never did ignore the
developments of others.

> So yes, continuing development/support of "a game labelled Nethack
> [tm]" is important.

I agree, it is. But how can this be accomplished, when the DevTeam
doesn't release a new version?

> Unfortunately, I feel that the DevTeam neglects Nethack.
> Check the bug list at http://www.nethack.org/v343/bugs.html and
> http://www.nethack.org/v343/spoiler.html.
> If you consider 5 years without a single bugfix release when there is
> a metric crapload of bugs, most of them marked "fixed" reasonable,
> I'll have to respectfully disagree.
>
> In my humble opinion, the right things for the DevTeam to do is (a)
> state whether they are still supporting Nethack or not, and (b)
> release the bug fixes that have been implemented and tested as 3.4.3a
> (or 3.4.4).

A bugfix release would have been nice. Long time ago.

If you watch the #nethack channel on freenode, there are a lot of
requests for restoring crashed games. 

> Continuing silence is bad for the game and for the community.

Yes.

But the DevTeam doesn't have any obligation to do anything.

The current code base is indeed better than a lot of commercial games
that get sold in a hardly playable state and even with patching don't
work satisfactorily.

And NetHack *is* open source. Everybody can do it themselves if they
want.

Bye
Patric

